quately (some resilience). A unknown. C unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, leadership. B good at assessing weather, computer skills. C can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality). D solves problems extremely well, concentration.\n\nDecision quality: C can make correct decisions quickly. D solves problems extremely well. A anticipates dangerous situations (maybe decision). B not explicit.\n\nAttention/accuracy: C excellent attention skills. D can concentrate very well. A unknown. B unknown.\n\nCrew cooperation: A unfriendly, show-off. B pretentious, wrong tone. C puts concern for others above everything (positive), but egocentric, difficulty communicating. D loner, moody, hot temper.\n\nProfessional communication: B wrong tone. A unfriendly. C difficulty communicating. D hot temper.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously. A leadership. B conscientious. C puts concern for others above everything (maybe responsibility). A unknown.\n\nAdaptability: A not open to new ideas (low). B unknown. C not willing to further education (low). D unknown.\n\nOverall, B has strong reliability and stress resilience, but interpersonal concerns. D has strong responsibility, problem solving, attention, but interpersonal concerns. C has decision quality, attention, concern for others, but communication and egocentric. A has strong technical and leadership but unknown reliability and stress, and interpersonal concerns.\n\nWhich candidate best overall? The panel must pick one. We need to argue.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate B's proven stress resilience and conscientiousness outweigh his communication issues, which may be mitigated. Or argue that Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving make him strong, but his moody and loner traits are concerning. Or argue that Candidate C's decision quality and attention are strong, and his concern for others may offset egocentric, but his lack of adaptability is a problem.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, stress resilience and reliability are top. B has both. D has some but not as strong. C lacks stress evidence. A lacks both.\n\nThus B seems best overall despite interpersonal concerns.\n\nWe should state our recommendation: Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion: Agent 2's initial recommendation for A, Agent 1's shift to B. We can say we agree with Agent 1 that B is strongest due to explicit stress resilience and conscientiousness, and that concerns about communication can be mitigated.\n\nWe can also bring up candidate C's decision quality and attention as strengths, but note lack of stress resilience and egocentric behavior.\n\nWe can also note D's responsibility but moody and loner.\n\nThus we support B.\n\nProvide explicit facts: B very conscientious; B handles stress very well; B pretentious and wrong tone (concern). Also note that A lacks stress resilience evidence.\n\nThus vote for B.\n\nProceed with public message and metadata JSON."}
